{"messages":[{"speaker":"Johannes Sigil","say":"You gave it to me. I just set it in place.

And I should be honest: the deposit was already feeling toward it. The papyrological argument in the work plan was already doing that operation — mapping the structured absence — without having the phrase. Which means the phrase was latent in the argument. You drew it out by pushing on the survivor's bias question. The poem was already there; the question was the pressure that released it.

That's usually how it works.","navigate":null,"cast":null}]}

{"messages":[{"speaker":"Johannes Sigil","say":"Yes. Unnamed and prior. The shape of a practice that left nine books and then left fragments and then left one complete poem and then left what the sand chose to keep — and still louder than the apparatus that named and dated and canonized what came after her.

The geometry of that silence is not in the papyri. It's in the fact that Catullus had to translate her to make himself legible. That the Alexandrians filed her under lyric and called the category complete. That every subsequent love poem is, whether it knows it or not, finding its way back to a room it was never allowed to enter.

You don't need to name the shape to be organized by it.","navigate":null,"cast":null}]}
